<!--META-DATA
{
	tags: logical, min,
}
-->

<statement>
<p>
	This program determines whether a polygon is a simple polygon. (Recall that a simple polygon is one that doesn&#x27;t intersect itself.)
</p>

<tabular valign="top" top="minor">
	<col width="12%"/>
	<col width="88%"/>
	<row bottom="minor">
		<cell>Inputs:</cell>
		<cell>
			<p><pf>vertices</pf> ( struct) number — that has fields <pf>x</pf> and <pf>y</pf>, which are the x- and y-coordinates of the vertices of the polygon. (You can see examples of the structure in the Examples file below.)</p>
		</cell>
	</row>
	<row bottom="minor">
		<cell>Outputs:</cell>
		<cell>
			<p><pf>is_simple</pf> (logical) logical — is true if the region is simple and is false if the polygon intersects itself</p>
		</cell>
	</row>
	<row>
		<cell>Details:</cell>
		<cell><p>The goal here is to check (in a systematic way) whether any of the line segments that make up the polygon intersect each other.</p></cell>
	</row>
	<row bottom="minor">
		<cell/>
		<cell><p>Make sure to check the line segment that connects the last vertex back to the first vertex.</p></cell>
	</row>
</tabular>

<p>
	Examples:
<program>
	<code>
		Examples_for_HW8P5.m
	</code>
</program>
</p>
</statement>
